  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output A.1. More accurate and sustainable statistics on decent work using the latest statistical standards</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Continuous Multipurpose Household Survey (CMHS) was conducted for the 1st quarter 2021.  The Survey factored in statistical standards and methodologies outlined in the 19th ICLS towards an improved questionnaire that was administered in the survey. The questionnaire used in the survey integrated improved definitions of employment and unemployment. The ILO facilitated training for Labour Statistician of Statistics Botswana on provisions of the 19 ICLS, with particular focus on improved definitions of employment and unemployment that were factored into the design the labour force survey. 

 In addition, the ILO provided technical support was provided in the design of the questionnaire used in the survey as per the new statistical standards and, facilitated  a data production workshop in March 2021 for multiple stakeholders involved in the production of labour market indicators including labour statistics Officials from Statistics Botswana.  The workshop facilitated the participants&apos; review of decent work and SDG labour market indicators and sensitized them to the definition, formula and source of each indicator, and on how to interpret the indicators and on how they can be used for policy. Participants also learned about the recommended disaggregation of indicators (such as sex, geographical location age groups), and learned how to interpret data based on the various disaggregation.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">A.1.1. Number of member States that strengthen their labour market statistics, standards and information systems through improved statistical surveys and use of other statistical sources</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
